Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:02.470 align:start position:0%
 
now last weekend the mayor of greater  manchester said  that northern england is in a hotel  california lockdown and it doesn't look  like anyone  is going to be able to leave anytime  soon millions of people are already  living under postcode lockdowns  including people in steli bridge in  greater manchester where we're joined  from there by labour's shadow work and  pension secretary jonathan reynolds  thank you very much for  being on the program mr reynolds so on  monday  the government is reportedly going to be  introducing this three-tier  lockdown system where people in the  third tier which could well include  people in your own constituency  will face the toughest level of  restrictions potentially closing  pubs stopping households mixing anywhere  is that something labor supports  good morning sophie look the first thing  i just have to put on the record  is the level of anger and frustration  that exists in towns and constituencies  like mine the way this is happening so  there's been  leaks to newspapers there's been no  consultation with local leaders this is  not the way  to do things people feel they're being  treated with contempt and not with  respect and that is simply not good  enough  to answer your question directly the  fact is there are going to have to be  further measures and the reason for that  is the government have lost control  right they've lost control of the  messaging they've lost control of  uh test and trace they've lost control  of the virus unfortunately  and that means we're going to have to  have further measures that wasn't  inevitable  that time we we got from the lockdown  and from the development of  what should have been an effective test  and trade system should have meant we  don't have to get to that position  but the crucial point is this further  restrictions  have to come with economic support the  situation for businesses here in my  constituency is not acceptable how can  they plan  for next week they've had nine weeks of  very very restrictive  local restrictions that's had a huge  impact on them of course it has  and now we find out we will learn  tomorrow what is going on so the prime  minister's got to get a grip of this  he's got to come to parliament but  crucially any further restrictions  must come with appropriate levels of  support for businesses and people i mean  to be fair the government has announced  more support for businesses and people  the chancellor said the government will  pay two thirds of people's wages  if businesses are forced to close i mean  this is unprecedented the government's  never stepped in before to pay people's  wages  um are you demanding too much  no it's not good enough what the  government have it out so let's be clear  for nine weeks now in constituencies  like this one the restrictions have been  that you cannot socialize indoors with  people who aren't your immediate  family so you add that to for instance  the curfew how can a restaurant or a  business  trade viably in that environment of  course don't forget  that we're now several so many months  into this crisis that all the businesses  have have the debt they have to get with  the bounce-back loans at the beginning  they've had very  uh poor trading for for most of this  year they're in a very difficult  position so no the support is not enough  and it must  get better but at the same time there  are other things the government could do  so we haven't yet got  sick pay for everybody the the payment  that was announced to 500 pounds for  people  self-isolating only about one in eight  workers are eligible for that  there's the money they underspend in the  grants that were given to councils that  could be  packaged up again and give councils the  power to give that out again so there is  much more  that needs to be done but you know  frankly we're in this position  because of the government's inability to  control this properly and i'm afraid  that does mean more restrictions for  people  and they deserve support if they're  asked to obey those restrictions going  forward  i'm interested in your insight as a  local mp  uh in an area that has faced these  additional restrictions as you said for  a long time 10 weeks or so and i spoke  to anti-birmingham last weekend and he  said he thinks that the government is on  the verge of losing  the north of england do you think that  there is a real concern now that people  might just stop  following the rules that people have  reached their breaking point reaching a  breaking point  it is a worry to be frank so at the  beginning you know we talked i think  in the country about how there might be  lockdown fatigue um  that is definitely more of a feature but  you know the reason for that sophie has  been  if you take the last two and a half  months here in greater manchester what  you've seen is  very significant restrictions on  people's lives can't see family members  can't visit  loved ones in care homes you know  businesses pubs can't trade properly  and at the same time no additional  support  and as well as that the infection rate  going from you know 50 infections  in a hundred thousand to in this area  now over 200 infections in a hundred  thousand and in central manchester  over 500 infections so people are  looking at it and saying  well we just don't think the  government's got a grip on this but we  do need them  to follow the rules you know we still  want the government to succeed we want  them to get  a grip on this but they're just not  doing that at the minute and part of  that is  you know how contracts have been handed  out for test and trace  getting nowhere near what we need to be  i i look you know just  over there to wales and and what they've  been able to do is far superior to what  we've got  in england so there are real lessons the  government have got to learn they've got  to get a grip on this because they are  starting  to lose people and there is resentment  to be frank there's resentment about the  north-south  divide in some of these local  restrictions and you can see that coming  across  so unless the prime minister gets a grip  of this it will get even more difficult  i'd appreciate a really clear answer on  this next question the 10 p.m rule where  pubs restaurants have to close at 10 pms  come in for an awful lot of criticism  some people saying it's  counterproductive  there's a vote on it next week does  labour support the 10 pm rule  well to be honest the 10 pm rule is  going to be rendered obsolete by what we  hear  tomorrow because we'll probably see  that's not that's  genuinely not what i asked as well does  labour back the 10 pm rule or oppose it  we think we have to be restricted to  hospitality yes but the crucial point is  there's got to be support with those  restrictions in terms of where  affections are  sorry i do apologize for coming in again  but i'm still  genuinely not clear does labour back the  10 pm rule or not  yes we back restrictions in hospitality  as long as they've got  the economic support that should go with  that but as i say i think what we'll see  on monday is  the situation will move on anyway for so  many businesses the crucial thing is  environment for business like a pub  right now it's not a viable one  and that is the problem that the  government has to solve so you'd vote  in favor of the 10 pm rule  it's not a vote specifically on the 10  pm rule it's a vote on all of the  package  of measures affecting areas like this  now when the infection rate is as it is  in a constituency like mine  you can't be arguing for less  restrictions that's just not feasible if  you look at where  that progress is going so it's not a  specific vote on the 10 pm rule  but on the package of measures overall  and as i say the reality is  we have got to accept that more measures  will happen because the government have  lost control of the situation the  crucial thing is  making sure there's the right level of  support to go with those restrictions  because at the minute there isn't  and that's what's not acceptable okay  now later on the program we're going to  be speaking to the housing secretary  robert generic  labour's calling for an investigation  into him what's that about  well basically the measures that  the department of communities local  government have been offering to local  towns the regeneration money  um robert generic is obviously in charge  of that as the as the secretary of  state that's a program that towns like  mine here in staley bridge very much  wanted to be  part of and we didn't get the money and  now we find out that where that money  has gone  has been very much directed by ministers  to what look like  uh conservative electoral battlegrounds  so we always knew when the data came out  you could compare  the different parts of greater  manchester for instance that got the  money and it wasn't clear why we didn't  get it but a place like cheadle down the  road did but now we know that ministers  have directly tried to influence that  they have  uh in robert generic's case sat on the  local board  that then made the application to and  then tried to cover that up so the whole  question  has always been of quite a murky one as  to how this money was allocated  the secretary of state has questions to  answer an investigation is the right way  forward

